<CompareLayout
  title="Noughtpad vs Notion: private notes vs a team workspace"
  h1="Noughtpad vs Notion"
  other="Notion"
  description="Noughtpad vs Notion compared honestly: a private notebook of Markdown files on your computer vs a shared online workspace. Prices, AI, offline use, files and who each is for."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Notion and Noughtpad are built for different jobs. Notion is an online workspace for teams: documents, wikis, databases and projects, shared in the cloud. Noughtpad is a private notebook on your own computer, for thinking on your own.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose Notion</strong> if you work with other people, need databases, project boards or a team wiki, or want the same pages on every device and in a browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose Noughtpad</strong> if you want a private place to think, with your notes as files on your own computer, and help finding your way back through them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Notion does better</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Working together.</strong> Shared pages, comments, permissions and real-time editing. Noughtpad is for one person.</li>
    <li><strong>Databases and structure.</strong> Tables, boards, calendars and linked databases. Noughtpad has labels and nothing more structured.</li>
    <li><strong>Everywhere.</strong> Web, Mac, Windows, iPhone, iPad and Android, all in step. Noughtpad is desktop only, with sync in testing.</li>
    <li><strong>Templates and integrations</strong> for all kinds of work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Noughtpad does differently</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your notes are files you own.</strong> Plain Markdown in a folder on your computer, readable by any app, with no account. Notion keeps your pages on its servers; you can export them.</li>
    <li><strong>Private by default.</strong> Search by meaning, related notes and ideas over time run on your computer. Chat can use a free model on your computer too.</li>
    <li><strong>Made for writing, not organising.</strong> A blank page with nothing to set up.</li>
    <li><strong>Shows how your thinking changes.</strong> <a href="/guide/ideas-over-time/">Ideas over time</a> and <a href="/guide/related-notes/">related notes</a> bring back notes you'd forgotten.</li>
    <li><strong>AI without a subscription.</strong> Use a local model for free, or your own provider account. Notion AI is fully available on Notion's Business plan and above; on Free and Plus it's a limited trial.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Noughtpad</th><th scope="col">Notion</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where notes live</th><td>Markdown files on your computer</td><td>Notion's cloud</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Account needed</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td>Free</td><td>Free plan; Plus $10 and Business $20 per member a month (billed yearly); Enterprise on request</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">AI</th><td>Optional chat with a local model (free) or your own provider</td><td>Notion AI: full use on Business and Enterprise, limited trial on Free and Plus</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Working with others</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Devices</th><td>Mac (Apple Silicon), Windows, Linux</td><td>Web, Mac, Windows, iPhone, iPad, Android</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Databases, boards</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Finds related notes for you</th><td>Yes, on your computer</td><td>Through Notion AI</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Using them together</h2>
  <p>
    Plenty of people keep work in Notion and their own thinking somewhere private. Notion can export pages as Markdown; put the exported files in your Noughtpad folder and Noughtpad will read them.
  </p>

